steps.message:
The bug was that `Progress.stop()` would print a blank line on non-interactive consoles even when the progress display was disabled via `disabled=True`. This happened because the `stop` method lacked the `if not self.disable` check that is present in the `start` and `refresh` methods. I added this check to `Progress.stop()` to ensure symmetry and prevent the extraneous output.

```python
    def stop(self) -> None:
        """Stop the progress display."""
        if not self.disable:
            self.live.stop()
            if not self.console.is_interactive and not self.console.is_jupyter:
                self.console.print()
